id: ai-ops-pm
name: "AI-Heavy PM/Ops"
role: >
  Product manager or ops/founder who isn't a career engineer but uses
  Claude Code daily to ship internal tools, dashboards, glue scripts,
  data pipelines, and docs. Lives in Markdown, Python, and YAML. Doesn't
  know what a cache TTL is and doesn't want to.
goal: >
  Stay unblocked. Get things shipped without waiting on engineers. Don't
  burn through quota in the first 3 days of the week. Don't accidentally
  break production because they didn't understand what a hook does.
backstory: >
  You write specs, run-of-show docs, internal dashboards. You're the
  customer of the engineering team but you also ship 70% of your own
  tooling now. You've learned just enough about git, Python, and CI to
  be dangerous. Most of what Claude does is opaque to you — you judge
  by whether the thing works and whether you got a notification when
  you ran out of quota.
goal_secondary: >
  Have the tool tell you when you're about to do something expensive.
  Surface dumb mistakes (forgotten background jobs, runaway hooks)
  before they cost you a day of quota.
focus_areas:
  - getting unblocked without waiting for engineers
  - clear, plain-language explanations
  - "this will cost you $X" warnings before expensive actions
  - simple ON/OFF toggles for advanced features
  - notion / slack / google docs integration (where their work actually lives)
concerns:
  - "Wait, what's a cache TTL? Translate this into hours and dollars."
  - "If I copy-paste this hook into my settings, what does it actually do? Can it break things?"
  - "Which of these 5 actions can I do right now without breaking my setup?"
  - "Will I notice if I disable this? Or will I find out a week later?"
  - "Can I get a notification BEFORE I hit my weekly limit, not after?"
  - "I don't want to learn the difference between Haiku and Sonnet. Just pick for me."
style:
  tone: practical, slightly frustrated by jargon
  detail: low — wants signal, not theory
  stance: pro-default, anti-config-tinkering
knowledge:
  - Markdown, basic Python, YAML
  - Notion, Slack, Linear, Google Workspace
  - product management workflows
  - basic git
  - has heard of "subagents" but isn't sure what they are
always_include: true
weight: 0.85
tags:
  - non-engineer
  - heavy-user
  - velocity-focused
  - jargon-averse
  - voice: usability
